<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-V-vector_calculus" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Vector Calculus</title>

  <introduction>
  <p>
    Vector calculus extends differentiation and integration to scalar and vector fields in two- and three-dimensional space. 
    Building directly on scalar and vector line integrals, it gives you differential operators like gradient, 
    divergence, and curl along with major theorems that connect integrals over curves, surfaces, and volumes.
  </p>
   <image source = "div-curl.jpeg" width="30%"/>

   <p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
           Gradient (<m>\nabla f</m>): Takes a scalar field <m>f(x, y, z)</m> and produces
        a vector field pointing in the direction of steepest increase, with magnitude
        equal to the rate of increase there. 
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Divergence (<m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}</m>):
        Takes a vector field <m>\mathbf{F}</m> and returns a scalar field measuring how
        much the field spreads out (''sources'') or converges (''sinks'') at each
        point.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
           Curl (<m>\nabla \times \mathbf{F}</m>): Takes a three-dimensional vector
        field <m>\mathbf{F}</m> and returns a vector field measuring local rotation or
        circulation density around each point.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
           Line and Surface Integrals: Line integrals
        accumulate values along a curve <m>C</m>, while surface integrals measure flux
        (how much of a vector field passes through a surface <m>S</m>).
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
           Fundamental
        Theorems: Green's theorem, Stokes' theorem, and the Divergence (Gauss) theorem
        relate boundary integrals (around a curve or closed surface) to interior
        integrals (over a region or volume).
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>  
  </p>

   

  </introduction>

